@lesson classic-nlp
Q: In TF-IDF, which words get the highest weight in a document?
- Words frequent in every document
* Words frequent in this document but rare across the corpus
- The longest words
- Stopwords
> \(\text{tf}\cdot\log(N/\text{df})\): high term frequency and high inverse document frequency.

Q: A term appears in 10 of 1,000 documents. What is its IDF with natural log, \(\ln(N/\text{df})\)? (two decimals)
= 4.61 ±0.02
> \(\ln(100)\approx4.61\).

Q: Perplexity of a language model is…
* the exponential of the average negative log-likelihood per token
- the number of parameters
- the accuracy on next-word prediction
- the size of the vocabulary
> Lower perplexity means the model is less surprised by the test text.

Q: TF-IDF with logistic regression is still a strong baseline for text classification.
= true
> It is fast, interpretable and often within a few points of fine-tuned models on topic and sentiment tasks.

@lesson word-embeddings
Q: What problem does negative sampling solve in word2vec?
- Out-of-vocabulary words
* The softmax denominator over the whole vocabulary is too expensive
- Polysemy
- Overfitting
> It replaces the full softmax with a few binary "real vs noise" comparisons per example.

Q: Which limitation do static word embeddings have that contextual embeddings fix?
* One vector per word, so "bank" (river) and "bank" (money) share a vector
- They are too large
- They cannot be trained on GPUs
- They only work for English
> Contextual models give each occurrence its own vector depending on the sentence.

Q: In embedding space, \(\mathbf v_{\text{king}}-\mathbf v_{\text{man}}+\mathbf v_{\text{woman}}\) lands near which word?
= queen
> Relationships such as gender are approximately consistent directions in the space.

@lesson tokenization
Q: What does each step of byte-pair encoding training do?
- Splits the longest word in half
* Merges the most frequent adjacent pair of symbols into a new symbol
- Removes the rarest word
- Assigns random IDs
> Repeating the merge builds a vocabulary of frequent subwords up to the target size.

Q: Why do byte-level tokenizers start from the 256 byte values?
* Any text in any script can be encoded, so there is no unknown token
- Bytes are faster to type
- It makes the vocabulary smaller than 256
- To remove Unicode
> Every string is a sequence of bytes, so coverage is guaranteed.

Q: Languages underrepresented in tokenizer training typically need more tokens per word. Which consequences follow? (select all)
* Higher API cost for the same text
* Less text fits in the context window
* Slower generation per word
- Better accuracy automatically
> Token count drives cost, context use and latency.

@lesson pretrained-nlp
Q: Which model family is pretrained with masked language modelling?
* BERT-style encoders
- GPT-style decoders
- n-gram models
- word2vec
> BERT hides about 15% of input tokens and predicts them from both sides.

Q: Which is the best fit for named-entity recognition on a new domain with a few thousand labelled sentences?
- Training a GPT from scratch
* Fine-tuning a pretrained encoder with a token-classification head
- TF-IDF on whole documents
- k-means
> Token-level heads on contextual embeddings are the standard, data-efficient approach.

Q: A typical learning rate for fine-tuning BERT-sized encoders is around…
- 1.0
- 0.1
* 2e-5
- 1e-10
> Small rates preserve the pretrained knowledge; a few epochs usually suffice.

@lesson speech
Q: What input representation do most speech models use?
- Raw text
* Log-mel spectrograms
- MFCCs only
- Images of waveforms
> Short-time Fourier transforms mapped to the mel scale and log-compressed.

Q: What problem does CTC loss solve?
* The unknown alignment between many audio frames and fewer output characters
- Background noise
- Speaker identification
- Tokenization of text
> CTC sums the probability of every frame-level path that collapses to the target.

Q: A reference has 10 words; the hypothesis has 1 substitution, 1 deletion and 1 insertion. What is the WER?
= 0.3 ±0.001
> \((S+D+I)/N=3/10\).

@lesson info-extraction
Q: In BIO tagging, what does B-ORG mark?
* The first token of an organization name
- Any token inside an organization
- A token outside any entity
- The last token of an organization
> B begins an entity, I continues it, O is outside.

Q: Why add a CRF layer on top of a BERT encoder for NER?
- To make inference faster
* To score whole tag sequences and avoid invalid transitions like O → I-ORG
- To replace the encoder
- To add more labels
> Learned transition scores plus Viterbi decoding give consistent sequences.

Q: What is the time complexity of Viterbi decoding for T tokens and K tags?
- O(T + K)
* O(T K²)
- O(K^T)
- O(T² K)
> Dynamic programming considers every previous tag for every current tag at each step.

Q: When extracting fields with an LLM, which practices matter? (select all)
* Validate the output against a JSON schema
* Measure field-level accuracy on a labelled sample
* Route low-confidence or invalid cases to a human
- Trust the output because it looks plausible
> LLMs can return confident but wrong values.

@lesson arabic-nlp
Q: Which normalization steps are common for Arabic search? (select all)
* Removing diacritics (tashkeel)
* Unifying alef variants (أ إ آ → ا)
* Removing the tatweel stretching character
- Deleting every vowel letter
> These remove spelling variation that carries little meaning for retrieval.

Q: In Arabizi, which digit usually stands for the letter ع?
= 3
> Arabizi uses digits that look like Arabic letters: 3 for ع, 7 for ح, 9 for ق.

Q: Why are character n-grams effective for dialects like Darija?
* Unstandardized spellings of the same word still share many character n-grams
- Dialects have no words
- They remove all ambiguity
- They need no training data
> Word-level features would see each spelling as unrelated.

Q: An English-centric tokenizer usually splits Arabic into more tokens per word than English.
= true
> That raises cost and shortens the effective context; measure it on your data.

@lesson exam
Q: You need sentence embeddings for semantic search in Arabic, French and English. What should you use?
- TF-IDF trained on English only
* A multilingual sentence-embedding model, checked on your own queries per language
- word2vec trained on English Wikipedia
- A GPT model's last-token logits
> Multilingual embedders map meaning across languages into one space; quality varies, so evaluate per language.

Q: Which pairs are correctly matched? (select all)
* BERT ↔ bidirectional encoder, masked LM
* GPT ↔ causal decoder, next-token prediction
* Whisper ↔ encoder–decoder for speech
- word2vec ↔ contextual embeddings
> word2vec gives one static vector per word.

Q: A model gets 90% accuracy on MSA test data but 60% on Darija comments. What should you do first?
* Collect and label in-domain dialect data, then evaluate and fine-tune per segment
- Report the average and ship
- Remove the Darija comments
- Increase the model's temperature
> Averages hide failing segments; dialect data closes the gap.

Q: What is the WER if the hypothesis has 2 substitutions and 2 deletions against a 20-word reference?
= 0.2 ±0.001
> \((2+2+0)/20=0.2\).
